      "reason": "valid",
      "signature": "-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niF0EABECAB0WIQQjejz276bPDxKN3p04/gJpOPlJlwUCaujCYgAKCRA4/gJpOPlJ\nlwT+AJ4sY/ntk9EMj1KY7ySmon/hNp5r5QCfS0j1aeVRxwY5yKjwbt0j58j6nfo=\n=yDEB\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----",
      "payload": "tree 7da166fa4bba47be605884a97944e3d0fdda00b9\nparent 87f6c8ca11a2a41c98328dcc69211c9c36c2d506\nauthor Yukihiro \"Matz\" Matsumoto <[redacted-email]> 1793630196 +0900\ncommitter Yukihiro \"Matz\" Matsumoto <[redacted-email]> 1793639010 +0900\n\nA class a reopened Object's body makes is named as a top-level one\n\nclass_ruby_name builds a class's Ruby name from its enclosing classes, and\na reopened Object was one of them, so `class Object; B = Class.new;\nS = Struct.new(:a); M2 = Module.new; end` named them Object::B,\nObject::S and Object::M2, where CRuby names Object's constants as the\ntop level's: B, S, M2. The walk now stops at Object. Only the identity\nkey of an anonymous class made there changes in the corpus C.\n\nFound while checking CodeRabbit's notes on PR #10774; master named them\nso too.\n\nRefs #10774\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <[redacted-email]>\n",
